   "notes": "All pages fetched 2026-10-12. Measurements: (1) Piffl et al. 2014, from RAVE survey stars: 533 (+54, -41) km/s at 90% confidence, plus a 4% systematic uncertainty. Here 'escape' means reaching three virial radii (3 R340). Wording discrepancy: the arXiv listing's abstract says '5% systematic'. That text is left over from v1, which gave 537 (+59, -43). The v3 PDF abstract and the published abstract both say 4%, so 4% is used. (2) Deason et al. 2019, from Gaia DR2 data plus cosmological simulations: 528 (+24, -25) km/s at 8.3 kpc from the Galactic centre, with the boundary at 2 r200. The abstract does not say what confidence level these errors are. (3) Monari et al. 2018, from Gaia DR2 (about 2,850 counter-rotating halo stars): 580 ± 63 km/s, boundary at three virial radii. It shares authors (Piffl, Steinmetz) and method with (1), so it is not counted as independent. (4) Koppelman & Helmi 2021, from Gaia (about 10^7 halo stars, sideways motions only): 497 ± 8 km/s. The authors call this 'most likely biased low, our best guess is by 10%' and say the true value 'most likely is closer to 550 km/s'. (5) Necib & Lin 2022, from Gaia eDR3 with a multi-component fit: 445 (+25, -8) km/s with two components (preferred), and 472 (+17, -12) km/s with one. Their arXiv v1 used Gaia DR2 and gave 484.6 (+17.8, -7.4); the published eDR3 value is used here. (6) 2025 LAMOST K-giant preprint, using Gaia DR3: 523.74 (+12.83, -13.47) km/s. The arXiv page shows no journal reference. Why 'verified': (1) and (2) come from different surveys (RAVE versus Gaia), teams and methods, and they agree within their errors (533 versus 528). (6) also agrees. Value used: 530 km/s, rounding (1) and (2). Range: the published central values run from 445 km/s (Necib & Lin 2022) to 580 km/s (Monari 2018). With error bars the span is about 437 to 643 km/s. Newer Gaia analyses tend to sit lower, at 445 to 525 km/s. The spread comes from how the tail of the fastest stars is modelled and from where 'escape' is set. The Galaxy has no sharp edge, so 'escape' means reaching a chosen far radius (3 R340 or 2 r200), and the Local Group (Andromeda) is ignored. What it ignores for the page: the speed is relative to the Galaxy's centre, not to the Sun or Earth. The papers assume the Sun circles the Galaxy at 230 to 240 km/s (Necib & Lin 230 ± 10, Koppelman & Helmi 232.8, Monari 240). So a throw along the Sun's orbit needs roughly 300 km/s relative to the Sun, and one straight against it roughly 765 km/s. The Sun's and Earth's pulls add only a little. These are rough figures, not sourced. A 3,000 km/s throw is about 6 times 530 km/s, and about 4 times even the worst case against the Sun's motion. So in this model it leaves the Galaxy in any direction, unless it hits something. Rough estimate, not sourced: the Galaxy's pull bends such a path by roughly (v_c / v)^2 ≈ (235/3000)^2 ≈ 0.006 rad, times a factor of a few, about a degree at most.",
